                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Welcome to Haunted Hotel: Stay in the Light, another Five Nights at Freddy's clone that doesn't manage to hold a candle anywhere near the game that it attempts to emulate. The game itself doesn't really rely on any scares, either; it's just pure \"strategy\", if you can even call it that, while trying to avoid Death as much as possible.\n\nThe basic premise here is that you have to keep Death at bay for all eight of your hotel guests and yourself. You check each of their rooms, scan them to see if Death is present. If he is, you turn their light on and off to keep him at bay, and if he isn't you press on to the next room. There's also a hall camera, but it seems to do nothing; it will show Death, and illuminate room numbers, but when you go there he is not in the indicated rooms. You can't leave the lights on for long, otherwise your power depletes too quickly. Once the power is gone or all of your guests are dead, it's game over.\n\nThat leaves us with just how nonsensical Haunted Hotel: Stay in the Light is. In FNAF if you observe certain cameras, you can usually tell where the enemies are going to go next. With this game, however, the only camera that is supposed to do that for you, doesn't. An additional kick in the butt comes with the knowledge that with each hotel guest death, your power drains at an absurd rate. On the second night within the span of a minute, two of my guests died and my battery went down to 50%.\n\nThere are additional items that you can buy in the shop with your in-game money once you complete a night, but they're not even worth the effort. The extra battery manages to tack on a couple of percent and that's it. The radar is a one-use item. The \"heal all guests\" is too expensive to even think about getting. There's also this terrible white static each time you flip to another camera that is quite frankly headache-inducing and it may trigger seizures in sensitive people.\n\nHaunted Hotel: Stay in the Light is a game that doesn't even follow its on rules. Well, it sets rules for the player and enforces those, but Death doesn't mind cheating and going straight into a room after you've come out of it and then proceed to kill off multiple guests before you can even think about trying to visit their rooms. Sometimes guests will die in a second or two of each other; there's no time to get to a room at all. The horrible white static flickering between cameras prevented me from trying to play this any further, as I didn't feel up to putting myself through a headache for something as bland as this.\n\n[h1]Rating: [b]1.0/5.0[/b] - It's Bad.[/h1]\nThe Horror Network [url=http://store.steampowered.com/curator/28221963/]Curator[/url] | [url=http://steamcommunity.com/groups/thehorrornetwork]Group[/url] Click for Gore\n\n[b]The developer provided a free copy of this game for review, through The Horror Network's Curation page.[/b]",
